ccdb - A Parallel Relative Debugger

INFO: 
 - Information related to compiling and building ccdb can be found in the INSTALL file.
 - Information related to maintaining this repository can be found in the maintainer-README file.
 - Information related to usage can be found in the docs/ directory.

1. OVERVIEW

 CCDB is a relative debugger. Unlike other debuggers, ccdb allows programmers to compare
 data from multiple programs as they are executing. This information can then be used
 as an aid in identifying the location of errors.

 CCDB is a conventional interactive parallel debugger. It can be used to debug parallel and
 sequential programs using interactive commands, such as setting breakpoints and displaying
 program state. Because ccdb is a parallel debugger, it will allow commands to be applied to
 sets of parallel processes as well as a single sequential program.

 CCDB is built on top of GDB, as it is a very versitile debugger that has widespread adoption.
 GDB provides the low-level debug services and ccdb interacts with GDB in order to enable
 comparative debugging. The syntax used in ccdb has been heavily influenced by the High Performance
 Debugging Forum's HPD standard.

2. CCDB v1.0 RELEASE NOTES

 To be added.

3. SUPPORTED PLATFORMS

 ccdb is built on top of guard which is designed to be a highly-portable system. 
 However, Cray is only supporting ccdb for Cray systems and documented guard
 commands may not be supported in ccdb. The user should always refer to the Cray
 documentation when using ccdb.

4. Bugs, Questions, and Comments

 CCDB is maintained by the PE Debugger Development group at Cray as a joint project 
 with Guardsoft Ltd. and Monash University. 

 All bug reports, questions, and comments for ccdb should be directed towards
 members of the PE Debugger Development group at Cray.

